1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:13,533
Here is the definition this course is built on. Deep learning uses simulated analog circuits to process information.

2
00:00:13,533 --> 00:00:20,466
The parameters of those circuits, their settings, are chosen by an optimization algorithm that is driven by data.

3
00:00:20,466 --> 00:00:28,933
The goal: the learned circuit has captured the relevant pattern in its training data, so it can solve problems on new data.

4
00:00:28,933 --> 00:00:36,800
Start with the smallest circuit, Rosenblatt's adaptive neuron. Each input passes a knob, a weight, that scales it.

5
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:48,200
Add the scaled inputs and a bias. With these settings, the input 2 and 1 gives 0.8 times 2, minus 0.5 times 1, plus 0.2.

6
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:55,100
A threshold turns the sum into an answer: 1.3 is positive, so the circuit says plus one.

7
00:00:55,100 --> 00:01:02,266
Turn the knobs and the same circuit says minus one instead. The parameters are the program.

8
00:01:02,266 --> 00:01:09,100
Deep means that information flows through several such elements, one after another, before it reaches the output.

9
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:18,466
Each element still just multiplies, adds, and takes a max: the earlier unit's pattern, repeated in sequence.

10
00:01:18,466 --> 00:01:24,133
Who sets the knobs? Data does. Twenty labeled points and a line from a random start.

11
00:01:24,133 --> 00:01:30,333
The line is where the sum is zero. Points on its wrong side are circled in red: ten mistakes.

12
00:01:30,333 --> 00:01:37,566
The perceptron update: take a mistake, add its label times its input to the weights, and its label to the bias.

13
00:01:37,566 --> 00:01:42,566
The line swings toward that point, and the mistakes drop from ten to four.

14
00:01:42,566 --> 00:01:50,666
Repeat. Now two mistakes. A step can also make things worse, and here the count jumps to six, but the updates keep correcting.

15
00:01:50,666 --> 00:01:58,800
After four updates, no point is misclassified. The knob settings came from the data, not from a person.

16
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:06,800
But the goal was never these twenty points. Draw a hundred fresh points from the same source, and the circuit gets most of them right.

17
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:13,300
The few mistakes sit near the boundary: the learned line is close to the true rule, not identical to it.

18
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:21,033
Now let the labels follow a different pattern. The same circuit is right about half the time: it captured one pattern, not every pattern.

19
00:02:21,033 --> 00:02:28,133
That is why the definition says a relevant pattern: learning is only as good as the match to new data.

